# Test: BFD plugin persists a Meticulous SHA1 TX sequence across a process
# restart via the shared zefs state store (database.zefs).
#
# VALIDATES: spec-bfd-5 AC-7 -- the SeqPersister writes the last-sent sequence to
#            the managed state store and a fresh ze process resumes from that
#            floor so the peer does not reject our packets as replays. Since the
#            zefs-persistence migration the sequence lives in
#            {ze.config.dir}/database.zefs (via internal/core/statestore), not a
#            loose <session>.seq file; persist-dir is a vestigial opt-in flag.
# PREVENTS:  a regression where the persister drops the pending flush, writes
#            nowhere on the filesystem/CLI path (statestore not registered there),
#            or fails to restore the sequence on restart.
#
# Method: a native Go fixture launches ze twice over the SAME ze.config.dir, so
# both runs share one database.zefs. The first run creates a Meticulous session
# and waits until a sequence has actually reached the store. It then SIGTERMs
# cleanly (Close flushes the sequence). The second run restores the persisted
# sequence -- the engine logs "bfd auth sequence restored ... seq=N" (N>0), the
# observable proof it survived.
#
# Every wait here is on a condition, never on a duration. The earlier version
# ran each ze for a flat 6.0 seconds and then SIGKILLed the daemon 5.0 seconds
# after SIGTERM. Both are fixed durations standing in for conditions
# (ai/rules/fix-dont-record.md) and both failed under load:
#   * the 6.0s run expired before startup finished, giving
#     "FAIL: first run did not reach 'bfd plugin running'";
#   * the 5.0s SIGKILL cut database.zefs mid-flush. zefs updates an entry in
#     place with one pwrite for the data and a second for the container CRC
#     (pkg/zefs/store.go flushInPlace), so a kill between them leaves the CRC
#     disagreeing with the data. That is the accepted, documented consequence of
#     the in-place design, and the storage
#     layer then moves the unreadable blob aside and recreates it
#     (internal/component/config/storage/blob.go), destroying the very sequence
#     this test asserts. ze is therefore never SIGKILLed on a passing path.
#
# // the pre-migration assertion read a <session>.seq file; that file
# no longer exists (the sequence persists to database.zefs via statestore). The
# load-bearing check is unchanged in intent -- the sequence must survive a restart
# -- and is now observed through the engine's restored-sequence debug log.

option=env:var=ze.bfd.test-parallel:value=true
# BFD listens on the RFC 5881 / RFC 5883 FIXED ports, so every concurrently
# running BFD test's daemon binds the same wildcard (0.0.0.0, 3784/3785) tuple.
# SO_REUSEPORT above lets them co-bind, and the kernel then HASHES each inbound
# datagram to one socket in the group (internal/component/bfd/transport/
# udp_linux.go applySocketOptions) -- so a control reply or a reflected echo meant
# for this daemon can be delivered to a sibling test's daemon instead. That is the
# kernel-global surface option=exclusive exists for: unique addresses cannot
# partition a port number the RFC fixes. Tests outside the group still run
# alongside these.
option=exclusive:group=bfd-ports



cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze-test fixture plugin/bfd-auth-meticulous-persist:timeout=40s
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stderr:pattern=persisted and restored across restart
